A||||6||fierosound||ECM kills fast idle - is this normal??||||1||
Z||000000||fierosound||09-01-2001||12:29 AM||||I don't get this.  My 3.4L idles fairly smoothly, drives very well, and has<BR>lots of power.  If I open the throttle partway, to create a fast idle,<BR>let's say 2500rpm or so with no load, the engine will seem like it's misfiring and<BR>the ECM brings the RPM back down almost to idle speed.  Is this normal?  The ECM shows no codes at all.  ALL the sensors are new.  I'm at a loss as to what may be the<BR>problem, or even where to start checking.  Any ideas??<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Primaris||09-01-2001||04:31 AM||||My 2.8 does the same thing sometimes.  I don't think this is normal.  I have read about similar problems being caused by the throttle position sensor.  It is not manufactured well and develops corrosion on 3 small contacts inside.  There is a way to cut open the sensor and soldier the connections but I have not tried this yet.  So I cannot say if this will solve the problem.  If you have a spare try it out or maybe someone else here will have a better answer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||09-01-2001||10:44 PM||||Is this a 3.4 dohc.  if so they have a rev limiter and does not allow rpm over 2500 rpm in park or nuetral, for automatic transmission setup.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forsalestart.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierosound||09-02-2001||12:16 PM||||3.4L pushrod with 5-speed manual trans. Fiero fuel management, sensors, ECM etc.<P>Thanks, that is why I was asking if this was normal.  I'd thihk if it were, 2.8s would do the same.  I don't it had this symptom when I installed this engine nearly a year ago.  It's just developed a strange idle (somtimes hunts) and I noticed this as well.||||reg||10
Z||000004||Formula88||09-02-2001||07:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by PBJ:<BR><B>Is this a 3.4 dohc.  if so they have a rev limiter and does not allow rpm over 2500 rpm in park or nuetral, for automatic transmission setup.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The rev limiter in neutral is a bit higher than 2500.  It's about 4000 (mabye 4500?).  At least that's the case on my 3400SFI.  Not that it matters in this case, just a heads up.  Either way, if you think that's the issue, you can get around that by driving the car, and putting it in neutral while coasting over about 35mph.  The neutral rev limiter won't kick in then, and you could rev all the way to redline, if you wanted.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||09-02-2001||07:46 PM||||Fierosound, the Fiero ECM's don't have ABM so you have something wrong.<P>Does it do this when you are driving or only when you rev it in neutral?<P>I assume you used a stock camshaft also?<P>A dead TPS would throw a code. Any codes?<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 09-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierosound||09-03-2001||05:21 PM||||I think I may have fixed it (fingers crossed). Changing the O2 sensor seems to have solved it.  I had no codes but my Helms manual indicated that an O2 sensor that sends a false high reading will tend to make the engine run lean, giving it a poor idle, missing etc.<P>I'll have to drive it abit now, and see what happens.<P>Thanks to all...||||reg||10
